WEBVTT

00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:13,820
Is the best test to show me if my cancer is stable or in revision?

00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:23,820
Well, there isn't a best test. It's a collaboration between many tests.

00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:37,820
So, you know, you go from the most invasive, like a Pat scan or a CT scan to the lesser side of invasive this.

00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:49,820
And MRI to go down that pathway of lesser invasive this ultrasound to the blood work.

00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:54,820
So, I don't want to do another pet scan, but I feel like I'm doing real well.

00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:58,820
So, the objective side of that testing would be blood work.

00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:06,820
And you look at the blood work as a whole. So, you look at, if I have no metastasis,

00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:13,820
then I would look at alkaline phosphatase, calcium levels, liver, other liver function tests, kidney function tests,

00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:16,820
look at anemia numbers.

00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:20,820
In the blood test, you would look for things like that.

00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:31,820
And then you could look at, if you showed positive tumor markers in the past, you could use that as a helpful indication of whether you're going in the right direction or the wrong direction.

00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:37,820
But there isn't anyone test that's going to say, yes, I'm in remission or yes, I'm doing greater.

00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:41,820
Or the opposite of, oh no, I'm doing much worse.

00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:44,820
You take each test gives you clues.

00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:53,820
So, you take as many data points as you possibly can and you formulate a decision, clinical decision making process.

00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,820
Plus you add subjective findings into that as well.

00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:02,820
So, that's how you make the best differential diagnosis.

00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:07,820
And that's how you best make the best clinical decisions.

00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:16,820
You know, I talked to somebody today who they said, it was an alternative oncologist's person is going to.

00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,820
So, they are recommending a keyboard pill that this person is on.

00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:30,820
And then they did a DNA test on the person of the DNA circulated tumor cell levels were elevated.

00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:35,820
Therefore, they said the oncologist said your cancer is getting worse.

00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:41,820
We need to increase and put you on a platinum based medication, chemotherapy, because the cancer is getting worse.

00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:43,820
And I was like, well, hold on a second.

00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:45,820
What was the other data?

00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,820
Was it solely based upon circulated tumor cells increased?

00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:56,820
And this person wasn't for sure, but said, yes, because they had just had a CT scan and CT scan was normal.

00:02:57,820 --> 00:02:58,820
There was no growth.

00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:02,820
And she said her blood work was all under control.

00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:10,820
Well, it's like, okay, we can't make major clinical decision making based upon one piece of data.

00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:12,820
I just don't think that's correct.

00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:15,820
I don't think that's fair to the patient.

00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:25,820
I mean, yeah, if you're going to change their supplements or something like that, but if you're telling them to go on a medication that could alter their life forever,

00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:35,820
based upon one piece of data, when other data show that they're doing well, I just don't think that's proper practice.

00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:54,820
It sounds like the entire Zoom call here, I'm kind of grabbing about other practitioners and I don't need to do that, but I want you to be wise enough to discern what is going to be right care for you.

00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:57,820
Because you're going to other practitioners.

00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:01,820
So I'm trying to help you discern these things.

00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:05,820
So best test to show me if I cancer stable, there isn't one.

00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:08,820
You look at all sorts of data points.

00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:15,820
So doing blood work, doing scans, doing MRIs, looking at subjective data.

00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:19,820
That's how the best way to know if you're going down the right path.

